<p>In 1810, in a jail in Fayetteville, North Carolina, a man who had been in the country for three years and could not speak its language covered the walls of his cell with writing. The jailer could not read a character of it. Neither could the sheriff, the lawyers or the local minister. The script ran right to left in a hand that had been trained for decades, and it was almost certainly the first Arabic most of those men had ever seen written by a living person.</p>

<p>That is how Omar ibn Said became a curiosity, and then a legend, and then a name in a court record. He was not a curiosity. He was a scholar from the Futa Toro who had spent twenty-five years in study before an army took him, and the writing on the wall was not a cry for help. It was a man using the only language he still had full command of, in a room where nobody could read him.</p>

<p>Twenty-one years later, in the same state, he wrote his life down. It is the only known autobiography written in Arabic by a person enslaved in the United States, and it begins not with his own story but with a chapter of the Quran about who actually owns the world.</p>

<h2>Between the Two Rivers</h2>

<p>His own account of his origins is one sentence long and completely specific: "My name is Omar ibn Seid. My birthplace was Fut T&ucirc;r, between the two rivers." The two rivers are the Senegal and the Gambia, and Futa Toro was the strip of land between them &mdash; in his childhood an emerging theocratic Islamic state, the Imamate of Futa Toro, and today part of northern Senegal.</p>

<p>He was born around 1770 into a wealthy family of the Fula people, the pastoral and scholarly ethnic group whose influence runs from Senegal to Nigeria. His father, by his own account, had six sons and five daughters; his mother three sons and one daughter. Later American writers, unable to imagine an educated African who was not a prince, decided his father had been a king. One 1905 annotator in the file that now sits in the Library of Congress wrote that he was "born in the region around Timbuctoo and the son of a King or Chief" &mdash; two claims that the autobiography does not support and one that the geography contradicts.</p>

<p>This is worth dwelling on, because the gap between the legend and the document is the whole story. White America needed him to be a fallen prince, an exotic exception who proved nothing about the people it had enslaved. He described himself as a scholar, which was both more modest and more dangerous: a scholar is proof that the society he came from produced institutions, curricula and teachers.</p>

<h2>Twenty-Five Years of Study</h2>

<p>The core fact of his early life is in his own words: "I sought knowledge under the instruction of a Sheikh called Mohammed Seid, my own brother, and Sheikh Soleiman Kembeh, and Sheikh Gabriel Abdal. I continued my studies twenty-five years."</p>

<p>Twenty-five years. He was not self-taught in the heroic autodidact tradition, and there is no reason to romanticise the absence of a school: the school was there, and he was in it. What he had was a full classical education in the Islamic scholarly tradition, in Arabic, in a region with a manuscript culture centuries old. The subjects recorded in the sources include mathematics, astronomy, business and theology. He would have memorised the Quran, learned grammar and rhetoric, and been trained in the legal reasoning that a scholar needed.</p>

<p>That is the part of his biography that most histories of American slavery were built to make unthinkable. He arrived in Charleston, South Carolina with a literacy that most of the men who bought and sold him did not have in any language, and with a script that nobody around him could read.</p>

<h2>The Army, the Ship, the Sea</h2>

<p>In 1807 an army came. His account is as flat as a court deposition: "Then there came to our place a large army, who killed many men, and took me, and brought me to the great sea, and sold me into the hands of the Christians, who bound me and sent me on board great ship and we sailed upon the great sea a month and a half, when we came to a place called Charleston in the Christian language."</p>

<p>He was about thirty-seven, and he was among the last Africans carried across the Atlantic before the United States outlawed the overseas trade at the end of that year.</p>

<p>In Charleston he was sold to a man he called Johnson, and his description of him is one of the most quietly devastating sentences in the genre: "a small, weak, and wicked man, called Johnson, a complete infidel, who had no fear of God at all." Then the reason he ran: "Now I am a small man, and unable to do hard work so I fled from the hand of Johnson." He was in his late thirties, a scholar, physically slight, put to field labour on a rice plantation. He walked.</p>

<h2>Sixteen Days</h2>

<p>He made it to North Carolina. Near Fayetteville he saw what he called "some great houses" &mdash; churches &mdash; and on the new moon he went into one to pray. He prayed the way he had prayed his whole life, and a boy saw him and rode to fetch his father.</p>

<p>They came on horseback with dogs. "They took me and made me go with them twelve miles to a place called Fayd-il, where they put me into a great house from which I could not go out. I continued in the great house (which, in the Christian language, they called jail) sixteen days and nights."</p>

<p>Note the phrasing: <em>in the Christian language, they called jail</em>. He is annotating the vocabulary of his captors as he goes, the way a learner does when the word for a thing is itself an outrage.</p>

<p>On the sixteenth day the door opened and a crowd of men asked him his name &mdash; "What is your name? Is it Omar or Seid?" &mdash; and he did not understand them. Someone had read the walls. A man called Bob Mumford, identified by later scholars as the county sheriff or the clerk of court, led him out. He stayed four days. Then Mumford's son-in-law, a Bladen County planter named James Owen, asked whether he would come to a place called Bladen, and he said yes.</p>

<p>He was bought. He remained enslaved for the next fifty-four years.</p>

<h2>The Book That Taught Him English</h2>

<p>Here is where a man with no access to instruction did something learners today would recognise as a method.</p>

<p>The Owen family, impressed by his literacy, bought him an English translation of the Quran &mdash; a text he already knew by heart in Arabic. Later, with help from Francis Scott Key, they obtained an Arabic translation of the Bible, purchased for him in 1819. Its restored cover is now held at Davidson College, with his Arabic annotations inside.</p>

<p>Consider what those two books gave him. The English Quran was a parallel text: a document whose content he knew completely, in a language he was trying to learn. That is comprehensible input with a built-in answer key, and it is the same logic behind every bilingual edition, every graded reader and every dual-subtitle setup now sold to learners. He was not reading to find out what the book said. He already knew. He was reading to find out what English did with it.</p>

<p>And on the other side, by reading the Bible in Arabic he could follow the Christian scripture that his enslavers read aloud, in the language he still thought in. He joined the First Presbyterian Church in Fayetteville in 1820.</p>

<h2>The First Page</h2>

<p>In 1831 he wrote his autobiography. It runs to twelve leaves, in Maghribi script, on white wove paper, and it was written in North Carolina by his own hand.</p>

<p>The first three leaves are not autobiography. They are Surah al-Mulk, chapter 67 of the Quran, written out complete from memory: <em>Blessed be He in whose hands is the Kingdom and who is Almighty; who created death and life that he might test you.</em></p>

<p>Then, on the seventh leaf, the life begins: "My name is Omar ibn Seid. My birthplace was Fut T&ucirc;r, between the two rivers."</p>

<p>Scholars have made a great deal of that opening, and rightly. Al-Mulk is the chapter on divine sovereignty &mdash; on who owns the kingdom. A man writing his life story while legally owned by another man chose to begin with a text asserting that ownership belongs to God alone, and then wrote a narrative that never once describes himself as a slave who was content. To his American readers, the document reads as gratitude: he praises the Owens, praises their food and clothing, says he has known no want in twenty years. To a reader who knows the Quran, the frame is doing something else entirely.</p>

<blockquote>"My name is Omar ibn Seid. My birthplace was Fut T&ucirc;r, between the two rivers. ... I continued my studies twenty-five years."</blockquote>

<h2>What He Had Forgotten</h2>

<p>The most painful document in the collection is not the autobiography. It is the covering letter.</p>

<p>He wrote to a man called Sheikh Hunter, who had asked for the life story, and apologised for it before writing a word: "I cannot write my life because I have forgotten much of my own language, as well as of the Arabic. Do not be hard upon me, my brother." And again: "You asked me to write my life. I am not able to do this because I have much forgotten my own, as well as the Arabic language. Neither can I write very grammatically or according to the true idiom."</p>

<p>This is a man who had studied for twenty-five years apologising for his Arabic at sixty. The cause is not age. It is that he had been living for twenty-four years in a place where no one spoke his first language and almost no one could read his second. There was no one to talk to in Fula. There was no one to correct his Arabic prose. He had a Bible to read and letters to write, and that was the whole of his linguistic diet.</p>

<p>Language attrition is usually discussed as an inconvenience for people who move abroad for work. For Omar ibn Said it was the deliberate, structural consequence of being owned: a man cut off from every speaker of his own language, in a country where literacy in his script was treated as a phenomenon to be exhibited.</p>

<p>What survived is remarkable. His written Arabic held well enough that scholars can trace the Quranic allusions, the genre conventions and the double address across nearly two centuries. But he could feel the loss, and he said so, twice, in the same letter.</p>

<h2>The Two Audiences</h2>

<p>His conversion to Christianity is the most contested fact in his biography. He joined the Presbyterian church in 1820, he read the Bible in Arabic, he wrote Christian prayers, and he was described by white contemporaries as a devout convert.</p>

<p>He also wrote, in his own Bible, dedications to Muhammad. There is a card dated 1857 in his hand bearing Surat An-Nasr &mdash; a chapter about people entering Islam in multitudes &mdash; and on the back, in someone else's English handwriting, a note misidentifying it as the Lord's Prayer and attesting that Omar was a good Christian.</p>

<p>In the autobiography, he praises Muhammad when describing his life in Futa Toro. He calls Jesus <em>the Messiah</em>, which is the Quran's own title for him, used eleven times in the Quran, and calls him <em>sayyiduna</em>, the standard Islamic honorific for a prophet &mdash; not <em>rabb</em>, Lord.</p>

<p>Modern scholarship reads the text as written for two audiences at once: the white readers who wanted a conversion narrative, and Muslim readers who would recognise the Quranic framing and understand a man speaking under constraint. Basima Kamel Shaheen argues that the ambiguity was cultivated deliberately &mdash; that by refusing to resolve it, he made a wide readership confront what slavery had actually done to a scholar.</p>

<p>That reading is an interpretation, and it should be labelled as one. What is not interpretation is the textual fact: the document is saturated with Quranic structure, and the parts that read as accommodation sit inside a frame that asserts God's sovereignty over human beings. You do not need to decide what was in his heart to notice what is on the page.</p>

<h2>Fourteen Manuscripts</h2>

<p>He wrote at least fourteen works in Arabic: short chapters of the Quran copied by hand, now in the North Carolina Collection at UNC Chapel Hill; a letter from 1819 to Major John Owen full of Quranic quotation and geometric figures whose purpose is still debated, now at Andover Newton; prayers; and the autobiography.</p>

<p>He lived into his nineties, still enslaved, in the Owen household &mdash; at the Cape Fear River estate called Milton and later in Wilmington &mdash; and he was offered chances to return to Africa. He declined, saying he could not be sure his family and his people were still there. He died in 1864 and was buried in the Owen family graveyard in Bladen County. A neighbour's note in the collection calls him "Uncle Moro"; other documents call him Prince Omeroh. Neither was his name.</p>

<p>The manuscript travelled for a century and a half through private collections and disappeared for nearly fifty years before resurfacing. The Library of Congress bought the collection at Sotheby's in London on 30 June 2017, digitised all 42 documents, and put them online in January 2019. In 1991 a mosque in Fayetteville renamed itself Masjid Omar ibn Sayyid. In 2022 Rhiannon Giddens and Michael Abels premiered an opera based on his life at Spoleto Festival USA in Charleston &mdash; the city where he had been sold &mdash; and it won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 2023.</p>

<h2>What the Method Teaches</h2>

<p>Strip away the legend and what remains is unusually practical, because his learning conditions were so extreme that the techniques he used have to work.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>He learned the second language through a text he already knew by heart.</strong> The English Quran let him read for form while the meaning was already secure. If you are learning a language, a bilingual text of something you know cold is the highest-leverage reading you can do: no comprehension load, all attention on how the language builds.</li>
  <li><strong>He kept both scripts alive by using them.</strong> Arabic in his Bible annotations, his letters and his autobiography; English in the prayers and the church. The languages he stopped using are the ones he apologised for.</li>
  <li><strong>He wrote himself into the record.</strong> The single most quoted line in the whole collection is four words of self-identification. When nobody else will describe you accurately, a first-person document is not vanity. It is the only surviving evidence.</li>
  <li><strong>He used the register his audience could not refuse.</strong> His public argument, where he made one, was made in the vocabulary of the people who owned him &mdash; scripture, in their translation, quoted accurately. That is the same skill Tiyo Soga used in a Cape newspaper: learn the powerful language well enough to beat its speakers on their own textual ground.</li>
  <li><strong>He treated literacy as portable property.</strong> Everything was taken from him &mdash; his country, his name, his freedom, his family, his language. The one asset that could not be confiscated was a script he could write on a jail wall with a nail, and it was the thing that got him out of the cell.</li>
  <li><strong>He did not hide the loss.</strong> "I have forgotten much of my own language, as well as of the Arabic." Most learners hide attrition out of shame and lose the chance to repair it. He wrote the loss down, dated it, and sent it to a scholar who could see exactly what had gone.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>

<p>Omar ibn Said spent twenty-five years becoming a scholar, was captured at thirty-seven, and spent the next fifty-four years as another man's property. In that time he learned a new language in his forties, read two scriptures in each other's languages, wrote at least fourteen manuscripts, and produced the only Arabic autobiography by an enslaved person in American history.</p>

<p>The document that survives is short and strange, and it is easy to read past. Its first three leaves are not about him at all. They are about who owns the kingdom. Only after that does he say his name, his birthplace, the rivers.</p>

<p>He also told us what it cost: the language he thought in, half gone, at sixty, with nobody left to speak it to. Learners today call that attrition and treat it as a scheduling problem. For him it was the arithmetic of captivity, recorded in his own hand, in the script that got him out of the cell.</p>

